ic  but he has then ruled out joining a  liquid-led government  after the election another right-wing  challenger is gideon tsar  he's the leader of the new hope party  which supports settlement construction  in the occupied west bank  and opposes the iran nuclear deal  his political agenda is similar to  netanyahu's but he's  not interested in a coalition  partnership  and finally there's tv host turned  politician yeah lapid he heads the  opposition centrist  yesh ati party he is considered one of  the strongest candidates  and was once a coalition partner in a  netanyahu-led government  back in 2013. let's go to dalia  schindlin who is a public opinion  strategist she joins us from tel aviv  many thanks for speaking to us dalian on  al jazeera  now prime minister benjamin netanyahu is  hoping that the country's fast vaccine  rollout will push him into a stable  majority  but not everyone is convinced about how  he's handled that corona virus pandemic  are they  no in fact the vaccination rollout as  successful as it has been  is only the most recent thing to happen  in a year of  his trying to manage the pandemic in  ways that have drawn  a lot of fire including from some of his  own  supporter constituencies but i have to  say that for the most part  it seems like the majority of voters are  filtering  their assessment of netanyahu's handling  of the pandemic through  their pre-existing views of him as prime  minister  so there's plenty of criticism of him  from those who want  uh you know a government that does not  include the ultra-orthodox parties who  uh support a more liberal approach to  israeli society and they think that he  has given in  too much to the ultra-orthodox  communities uh in  imposing sweeping lockdown measures  presumably for political reasons even  though the ultra-orthodox community had  much higher rates  of infection through covet that is the  main point of contention  over his handling of the pandemic and  those people have not changed their  minds in being angry at him  because of the vaccination rollout even  though they give him credit for it  naturally of course his supporters give  him lots of credit for the vaccination  roll out but they were already  supporting him  indeed picking up on that point is it  not extraordinary that people's minds  are so little change when it comes to  uh uh president uh prime minister  netanyahu i mean it's  it's remarkable he's be he's on trial on  charges of bribery fraud breach of trust  but still he he remains where he is in  terms of his popularity  his staying power is quite remarkable  i've worked as a strategist  on eight national campaigns in israel  and in about 15 other countries and it  is  unusual to see an incumbent in power  for 12 years consecutively a total of 15  years and his  uh his standing in israeli society such  as it is  in the sense that about 25 to 29 percent  of the voters have supported him in  recent elections is really quite  uh impressive as a political feat  however it's not terribly surprising  because we've had three elections in  very quick succession  and all of the factors that we're  talking about save for the covet  management which is a brand new issue  they were all in place even ahead of the  april 2019 elections  the investigations against netanyahu  were known it was clear that he would be  indicted and in the course of  2019 and 20 he was indicted and his  trial started but voters knew all of  that even going into the april 2019  election so it's not that surprising  that they haven't changed their minds  one way or another about whether they  support or oppose him  but this time as you correctly pointed  out a majority of israelis do  prefer that he not continue being prime  minister but because of our coalition  system  he may very well be sorry to interrupt  you this time round he does  face new challenges from right wing uh  contenders as well  how is that likely to play uh into any  election result  well it does seem like he has lost votes  if you look at the average of all the  surveys and the general trend in the  many many many surveys israel uh  israeli media have conducted during this  campaign he  he has 36 seats in the outgoing  parliament uh that is out of 120 making  him the biggest party  but in all surveys and in all averages  of all surveys he is not getting any  higher than 32  but in most surveys he is reaching  between 28 and 30 seats  now there could be many reasons for that  decline and we also might see  additional support for likud on election  day tonight  when the polls come out when the exit  polls come out which is something that's  happened in the past but it doesn't look  like he's going  to be able to repeat 36 seats and so  to some extent that is partly because of  these right-wing challengers but to be  honest  the right-wing challengers haven't done  all that well because he's still  you know in top place by double digits  both in both in terms of the number of  seats  he's getting in in knesset in the  parliament and  leading in terms of the percentage of  israelis who vote for him towering  over those right-wing challengers each  of them naftali bennett and  are barely getting between eight and ten  percent of the vote  and so they have sort of created an  alternative but  there is uh just a stalwart base of  support for likud and netanyahu  and i think that giron tsar looks like  he's  uh on the path that moshe khachlon took  just in the 2015 elections not that long  ago also trying to create a liquid  breakaway  also won 10 seats but then couldn't  survive politically and his party no  longer exists  really interesting to get your thoughts  dahlia shin lin public opinion  strategist speaking to us there  from tel aviv well we can turn to our  correspondent natasha ghonim who is live  for us in tel aviv  as well natasha you're out on the  streets what is the mood out there  voters what kind of turnout are we  seeing  well we're in tel aviv as you mentioned  and turnout is 10  lower than it was during the previous  election cycle at this point in time  if you look behind me there is a big  sign here that says march  23rd all of us it's in our hands  obviously an attempt to try to boost  voter turnout for people who may have  election fatigue after now having to be  been asked to vote for a fourth time in  two years  we spoke to voters in jerusalem earlier  in the day  and again this all boils down to a  referendum  on prime minister benjamin netanyahu  for those who are supporting netanyahu  they're probably  in good spirits because it's expected  that the status quo  will remain at the end of the night and  by that i mean  that right now netanyahu's likud party  is still expected  to be the largest vote getter not enough  to reach the threshold of a 61 seat um  in the knesset which is needed to form a  government  but still enough that from there  netanyahu would be expected to do what  he's attempted to do  three previous times and that is to form  a coalition government  for those who oppose netanyahu who say  he's been in office too long  who have told us that they believe that  the corruption trial  that he is facing it resumes next month  he is facing  a fraud breach of trust and bribery  charges  they say that they feel very hopeless  they feel that  they don't understand why despite more  than 50 percent of israelis who are  recently who were recently polled  saying they want netanyahu out why it  seems like the political limbo  will remain in israel nonetheless  they did come out and vote and it'll be  interesting to see  if voter turnout is high a veteran  pollster said  that he did not expect voter turnout  especially due to the pandemic  and perhaps due to election fatigue to  be as high as it was  in the previous round of elections that  was 71 percent  natasha kaneem there for us in tel aviv  many thanks

